<title>USA: SOYBEANS - Warmth, rain needed to mature U.S. crops.</title>
<headline>SOYBEANS - Warmth, rain needed to mature U.S. crops.</headline>
<dateline>CHICAGO 1996-08-29</dateline>
<text>
<p>Slow-developing soybean plants in the U.S. Midwest need warm nights, daytime temperatures in the low 80s (Fahrenheit) and regular rainfall throughout September to reach maturity on time, agronomists said.</p>
<p>&quot;Night temperatures are extremely critical in September,&quot; said Ellsworth Christmas, agronomist at Purdue University.</p>
<p>Without the warm temperatures, farmers can expect &quot;smaller beans and maybe fewer beans,&quot; he added.</p>
<p>Temperatures below 60 degrees will retard soybean growth and at 40 degrees the plant will stop developing for one full day, Christmas said.</p>
